<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Association of Southeast Asian Nations</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ASEAN</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) is a regional organisation founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> in August 1967 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It later admitted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, and Timor-Leste became its eleventh member in 2025. It promotes economic cooperation, regional stability and non-interference among its members, with a secretariat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/association-of-southeast-asian-nations.jpg" alt="Association of Southeast Asian Nations" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ASEAN_HQ_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The predecessor of ASEAN was the Association of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, formed on 31 July 1961 and made up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. ASEAN itself was created on 8 August 1967, when the foreign ministers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia, the Philippines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and Thailand signed the ASEAN Declaration at Saranrom Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. The Declaration aims to accelerate economic, social and cultural development, to promote regional peace and to maintain close cooperation with existing international organisations. Originally the association was founded to contain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, but it gradually reoriented itself to economic development and to preventing foreign interference in Southeast Asia as a whole.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> became the sixth member on 7 January 1984 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> the seventh on 28 July 1995, after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. Laos and Myanmar joined on 23 July 1997. A Cambodian coup in 1997 and internal instability delayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s admission until 30 April 1999. Timor-Leste joined on 26 October 2025 as the eleventh member. The first summit of heads of government was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in 1976, and the ASEAN Free Trade Area was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> on 28 January 1992. A charter, signed in November 2007 and launched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> on 15 December 2008, formally established ASEAN as a legal entity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>ASEAN is an intergovernmental forum of all 11 states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, with its headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. It operates on the principle of consensus and respect for national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, so that it cannot force a member state to change its domestic laws. The ASEAN Way, as scholars describe it, is an informal and personal working style that relies on compromise, consensus and consultation, and on quiet diplomacy that spares members public embarrassment. Every two years ASEAN organises the SEA Games, a sporting event meant to promote cultural exchange among its members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ASEAN” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien III abdicated in favour of his son Hassanal Bolkiah in 1967, and Brunei became fully independent on 1 January 1984, joining <b>ASEAN</b> and the United Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…new state normalised relations with Indonesia in its first year, and Indonesia came to back its entry into <b>ASEAN</b>: applied for in 2011, accepted in principle in November 2022 and, as announced on 27 May 2025, completed at the October 2025 summit in Kuala Lumpur as the 11th…</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1967 Indonesia was a founder of the <b>Association of Southeast Asian Nations</b> (ASEAN), whose secretariat is in Jakarta, and its size makes it the anchor of regional diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a founding member of the <b>Association of Southeast Asian Nations</b> (ASEAN) in 1967, whose Kuala Lumpur declaration of 1971 proposed the region as a Zone of Peace, Freedom and Neutrality, and it has been active in the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the Organisation of Islamic…</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…1988 uprising and the long detention of Aung San Suu Kyi, who received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1991, made Burma a focus of Western sanctions and human rights campaigns, while China, India and the <b>Association of Southeast Asian Nations</b> (ASEAN), which Myanmar joined in 1997, pursued engagement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A founding member of <b>ASEAN</b> in 1967, Singapore hosts the secretariats of APEC and the Pacific Economic Cooperation Council, belongs to the Commonwealth, the Non-Aligned Movement and the East Asia Summit and keeps diplomatic relations with more than 180 states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
